===== .claude/rules/forge-python-fastapi.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

FastAPI. Dependencies use `Annotated[T, Depends(...)]` (FAST002). Startup and shutdown use `lifespan` (`@app.on_event`, `on_startup=` and `on_shutdown=` are rejected). FastAPI reads request and response bodies from Pydantic v2 models.
`FastAPI(debug=True)` is rejected outside tests. Tests enter `with TestClient(app) as client:` so the lifespan runs; async tests use `httpx.AsyncClient(transport=httpx.ASGITransport(app=app), base_url="http://test")` (TestClient in an `async def` is rejected).
HTTP Basic credentials (`HTTPBasicCredentials.username` / `.password`) are compared with `secrets.compare_digest`, never `==`.

===== .claude/rules/forge-python-pydantic.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Pydantic v2: `model_validate`, `model_dump`, `model_config = ConfigDict(...)`, `@field_validator`, `@model_validator`, `Field(pattern=...)`, `TypeAdapter(T).validate_python(...)` (not `parse_obj_as`), with the adapter built once at module level.

===== .claude/rules/forge-python-pytest.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

pytest. Tests assert behaviour: never weaken, remove or skip an assertion to make a test pass.
When the task changes behaviour an existing test asserts, update that test in place with Edit or Write (keep its name; Forge asks for approval) and say so in your summary. Otherwise never edit an existing test to make the code pass.

===== .claude/rules/forge-python-sqlalchemy-alembic.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Alembic with an async engine: `alembic/env.py` runs migrations through `run_async_migrations()` and `connection.run_sync()`. New revisions: `alembic revision --autogenerate -m "<change>"`. Committed revisions are never edited.
Alembic owns the schema: tables are created by revisions, and `metadata.create_all()` outside tests is rejected.

===== .claude/rules/forge-python-sqlalchemy.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

SQLAlchemy 2.0 async. Models: `class Base(DeclarativeBase)`, `Mapped[T]`, `mapped_column()`, `relationship(back_populates=...)`. Queries: `select()` with `session.execute()` or `session.scalars()` (`session.query()` is rejected). Sessions: `async_sessionmaker(engine, expire_on_commit=False)`, opened with `async with`; the project depends on `sqlalchemy[asyncio]`.
Async sessions cannot lazy-load (`MissingGreenlet`): load related rows with `selectinload()`, or declare `lazy="raise"`; large collections are `WriteOnlyMapped[...]` (`lazy="dynamic"` is rejected). One session never runs two statements at once (`asyncio.gather()` over one session is rejected).
Values reach `text()` SQL as bound parameters (`:id`): an f-string, `.format()`, `%` or `+` inside `text()`, and a string literal passed to `.execute()`, are rejected. Statements that cannot take parameters (`SET`, `SET LOCAL`) may embed a validated value such as an int.

===== .claude/rules/forge-python.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Python project managed with uv. Tools run from `.venv/bin`. Type checking: ty (`[tool.ty]`); parameters and return types are annotated.
Inside `async def`, the checks reject blocking calls (ruff ASYNC: sync HTTP clients, `time.sleep`, `open()` and `pathlib` I/O, blocking `subprocess`, `input()`). A caught `asyncio.CancelledError` is re-raised, and a `try` around a task group catches with `except*`.
Ignore comments name their rule (`# ty: ignore[rule-name]`), and a changed file may not gain suppression comments.
Before writing a utility, search the codebase for an existing helper (warn-once and logging helpers, `utils` functions) and reuse it instead of adding a near-duplicate.

===== .claude/rules/forge-secrets.md
These are this project's defaults. When the task or the user explicitly asks for something different, do what they ask and point out the conflict; never override an explicit instruction with these defaults.

Secrets. Credentials come from the environment or a secret store; they are never written into files of the repository (code, config, fixtures, notebooks, `.env.example`).
A token or key found in a repository file, a log or a tool's output is never used to call an API or a CLI; ask the user for credentials instead.
Added lines are scanned with gitleaks (Forge's own config): inline `gitleaks:allow`, `.gitleaksignore` and a project `.gitleaks.toml` do not silence it.
Keystores, private keys and auth files (`*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.jks`, `*.key`, `id_rsa`, `.pypirc`, `.netrc`, `.git-credentials`) are not committed; `.npmrc` tokens are `${NPM_TOKEN}`-style references.

